---
// THE FIRST-VISIT GATE. Rendered from index.astro ONLY — never BaseLayout, so no other route can inherit a
// full-screen interstitial. See docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-01-first-visit-gate-design.md.
//
// THE ONE INVARIANT THAT MATTERS: this ships NOT COVERING, and JS raises it. The static HTML carries the gate
// in its dismissed state and an inline script adds .is-up. A click-gate that defaulted to covering would lock
// out every visitor whose JS failed, and would also hide the page from anything that does not run scripts.
// The page's full markup is in the HTML either way, so this is an overlay, not a content gate — which is what
// keeps it crawlable. Same trick as BaseLayout's descent-veil, which parks itself off-screen by default.
// tests/distSmoke.test.ts asserts the shipped HTML carries no covering class, so the invariant is checked
// against the build rather than trusted.
import { name, roles } from '../data/profile';
// THE LINES ARE THE ORIGINAL 21st.dev GEOMETRY, ported. Six candidates were built as real full-screen gates in
// /proto-gate — descent trails, combed contours of the hero's own field, and four black-hole readings — and the
// owner's verdict was "the original is the best". It is a BORROWED drawing rather than one this site derived,
// which is a real cost and is written up in lib/gateRefPaths.ts; it should carry a credit if it stays.
//
// What makes it work, and what every original attempt here failed to reproduce: the family CONVERGES (the
// offset enters the two halves of each curve with opposite sign, so the left end moves right while the right
// end moves left) and its opacity ramps all the way 0.1 -> 1.0, so most strokes recede and a reader picks out
// about eight over a haze. Our combed version structurally could not do the first part — its no-knot guarantee
// needs a tilt that exceeds the field's maximum gradient, which forces the strokes parallel.
import { GATE_COUNT, REF_POSITIONS, refFamily, refViewBox } from '../lib/gateRefPaths';
import { parseTrack, visibleWindow } from '../lib/gateComets';
// GATE_DRAWN_MIN and GATE_OPACITY_PEAK are handed to the inline script through define:vars rather than typed
// into it. The script cannot import — it must run before first paint — but the four numbers that decide whether
// strokes vanish are unit-tested in lib/gate.ts, and passing them keeps the test and the shipped behaviour on
// one value. Everything else in there is restated prose-and-arithmetic, which is the standing trade.
import {
  GATE_DISMISS_MS,
  GATE_DRAWN_MIN,
  GATE_OPACITY_PEAK,
  GATE_OPACITY_RAMP,
  GATE_OPEN_PHASE,
  GATE_REVEAL_EVENT,
  GATE_SEEN_KEY,
  GATE_UP_ATTR,
} from '../lib/gate';

// The two mirrored families stay SEPARATE, as two groups. The original renders <FloatingPaths position={1} />
// and position={-1}; keeping that split is also what makes the motion affordable — a group is one composited
// layer. Keeping the split is the source's own structure (it renders the component twice, at position +1 and
// -1) and the script's seed strides across both groups in one pass. NOTE: there is no transform on either group
// — an earlier version drifted them and was rejected as static, and this line still claimed "two transforms
// instead of 72 paint-bound path animations" afterwards. What ships is 48 per-path dash writes from one rAF.
// GATE_COUNT curves per family rather than the source's 36 ("less lines"), SMOOTHED at the join (the source
// kinks up to 32.8 degrees there), and the viewBox pulled back to the reference's own apparent scale ("the
// example i gave you zoomed out a bit compared to yours"). All three are gateRefPaths' business — see there.
const families = REF_POSITIONS.map((p) => refFamily(p, GATE_COUNT, true));
const viewBox = refViewBox();
const vb = viewBox.split(/\s+/).map(Number) as [number, number, number, number];

/**
 * EACH CURVE'S ON-SCREEN ARC WINDOW, computed here at build time and shipped per path.
 *
 * This is the other half of "entire lines go dark immediately", and the half no opacity curve can reach. Only
 * about a quarter of each curve is inside the frame, so a dash sweeping the WHOLE arc is off-picture for most of
 * its cycle: measured across the family, every stroke is entirely absent for 25-59% of its own cycle, mean 42%.
 * That is in the reference too — the owner spotted it himself and said it was less noticeable there.
 *
 * Clipping the dash's travel to this window fixes it at the cause while changing nothing else about the motion.
 * It is computed at BUILD time because the driver is inline (it must beat first paint) and so cannot import
 * lib/gateComets, and against the viewBox rather than the viewport because that is the conservative direction —
 * `meet` plus element clipping always leaves MORE visible than the box, so a dash held inside this window is
 * always on screen.
 */
const windows = families.map((fam) => fam.map((t) => visibleWindow(parseTrack(t.d), vb)));
const letters = [...`${name.first} ${name.last}`];
---

<div class="gate" data-gate role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="gate-name">
  <svg
    class="gate-field"
    viewBox={viewBox}
    preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"
    aria-hidden="true"
    focusable="false"
  >
    {/* MEET, NOT SLICE — which is also the thickness fix. The original leaves preserveAspectRatio at its
        default, and that default is meet. On a 1920x1080 screen meet scales the 696-wide box by 2.76 where
        slice scales by 3.42, so slice was drawing every stroke about 24% thicker than the source does at the
        same authored width. meet still fills the screen, because the geometry runs far outside the viewBox
        (y -189..875) and an SVG clips to its element rather than to its viewBox.

        NO vector-effect HERE ANY MORE, and that was a performance decision as much as a fidelity one. It pins
        the stroke to its authored CSS width regardless of scale, which made the lines hairline — but it also
        defeats the renderer's stroke caching, and with 48 full-screen paths that is not free. The original does
        not use it either; `meet` already removed the ~24% of extra weight that `slice` was adding, so the
        widths below are the source's own and scale the way the source's do. */}
    {families.map((fam, f) => (
      <g class="gate-fam" data-fam={f}>
        {/* data-len carries the curve's measured arc length in user units. The script below reads it and
            writes the dash itself, so nothing here depends on pathLength normalisation or on CSS being able
            to interpolate a two-value dasharray. With no JS the path is simply drawn whole. */}
        {fam.map((t, i) => (
          <path
            class="gate-trail"
            d={t.d}
            data-len={t.len}
            data-a={Math.round(windows[f][i].a * 10) / 10}
            data-b={Math.round(windows[f][i].b * 10) / 10}
            stroke-opacity={t.opacity}
            stroke-width={t.width}
          />
        ))}
      </g>
    ))}
  </svg>

  <div class="gate-text">
    {/* A <p>, NOT an <h1>, and that is a correctness fix rather than a style choice. The gate shipped with
        an <h1> first and tests/distSmoke.test.ts caught it immediately: "index.html has 2 <h1> elements; a
        document gets exactly one". The hero's name is the homepage's real heading and this is a loader on
        top of it, so the second <h1> was a duplicate outline, not a second section — and the a11y bar here
        is 100. The dialog keeps its accessible name because aria-labelledby may point at ANY element, so
        nothing about the announcement changes. */}
    <p class="gate-name" id="gate-name">
      {letters.map((ch, i) => (
        ch === ' '
          ? <span class="gate-space"> </span>
          : <span class="gate-letter" style={`--l:${i}`}>{ch}</span>
      ))}
    </p>
    <p class="gate-roles">{roles}</p>
    <button class="gate-enter" type="button" data-gate-enter>
      enter the descent <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    </button>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  /* PARKED, NOT COVERING. Default state is fully dismissed: no JS means no gate. */
  .gate {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 10000; /* above .descent-veil's 9999 — if both ever raise, the gate owns the screen */
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    background: var(--bg);
    opacity: 0;
    visibility: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .gate.is-up {
    opacity: 1;
    visibility: visible;
    pointer-events: auto;
  }

  .gate-field { position: absolute; inset: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; }
  /* THE STROKES ARE PAPER, NOT OCHRE, because the source draws them in `currentColor` under
     `dark:text-white` — white threads on a near-black ground. Ochre was my substitution and it is most of why
     the field read as muddy: the opacity ramp starts at 0.1, and a GOLD line at a tenth opacity over near-black
     has almost no luminance left to carry, where a near-white one still reads as light. `--paper` is the site's
     ivory rather than pure #fff, which keeps palette discipline without losing the original's effect. */
  /* THE THREE TOKENS THE STROKES MAY LEAN BETWEEN, declared here so the script can read them with
     getComputedStyle and never names a colour itself. That matters twice over: the palette rule allows only
     tokens from tokens.css, and these resolve per THEME — in dark, `--ochre` is the phosphor emerald, which is
     exactly what a dark-theme stroke should warm toward. The script reads them once at init; the theme toggle
     is `inert` behind the gate, so it cannot change under the animation. */
  .gate-field {
    --trail-base: var(--paper);
    --trail-warm: var(--ochre);
    --trail-cool: var(--indigo);
  }
  .gate-trail { fill: none; stroke: var(--trail-base); stroke-linecap: round; }

  .gate-text { position: relative; text-align: center; padding: 0 7vw; }

  .gate-name {
    font-family: var(--font-display), Georgia, serif;
    color: var(--paper);
    font-size: clamp(44px, 9vw, 116px);
    line-height: 0.95;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .gate-letter, .gate-space { display: inline-block; }
  .gate-space { width: 0.3em; }

  .gate-roles {
    /* Display italic, matching Heights.astro's treatment of the same string — the dissolve only reads as
       one screen if the words land in the same voice as well as the same place. The spec said mono; the
       hero reserves mono for the line BELOW this one, so matching the hero wins. */
    font-family: var(--font-display), Georgia, serif;
    font-style: italic;
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--paper) 62%, transparent);
    font-size: clamp(15px, 2.2vw, 21px);
    margin: 18px 0 0;
  }

  .gate-enter {
    /* The site's existing chip treatment (see sections/Signature.astro), not the reference's glassmorphic
       panel. There is no such vocabulary here and adding one for a single button is what the taste rules
       exist to prevent. */
    margin-top: 34px;
    font-size: 12.5px;
    padding: 10px 18px;
    border-radius: 9999px;
    background: transparent;
    /* A TOKEN, NOT A HARDCODED RGBA. This was rgba(239,233,221,0.8) — the LIGHT theme's --paper, frozen —
       while :hover below resolves var(--paper) live, so in dark theme the label changed hue on hover
       (#efe9dd -> #dfe3df). Signature.astro, the model this chip is borrowed from, uses text-paper/80,
       which follows the theme. Palette discipline is a taste rule here, not a preference. */
    color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--paper) 80%, transparent);
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ochre) 25%, transparent);
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: color 0.2s ease, border-color 0.2s ease;
  }
  .gate-enter:hover { color: var(--paper); border-color: var(--ochre); }
  /* :focus AS WELL AS :focus-visible. The script focuses this button programmatically, and Chrome does not
     match :focus-visible on a programmatic focus with no prior keyboard interaction — so the only control
     in a modal dialog had no visible focus indicator until the visitor happened to press a key. */
  .gate-enter:focus,
  .gate-enter:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--ochre); outline-offset: 3px; }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .gate { transition: opacity 600ms ease, visibility 600ms ease; }

    /* NO CSS ANIMATION ON THE STROKES — the dash is driven from the script below, the way the source does it.
       Four CSS translations of framer-motion's pathLength/pathOffset shipped here first and each produced its own
       artifact: a sub-unit stipple, an offset that never interpolated, a fixed-length dash that slid in and out
       of frame. Keep it in JS.
         VECTOR-EFFECT MUST STAY OFF, which is the one durable finding from the round where this "flashed".
       `vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"` stops the renderer caching stroked geometry, so every full-screen path
       is re-stroked at device resolution every frame. It arrived in the same commit as the dash, which is how it
       escaped blame for a while — the source animates a dash on just as many paths and runs fine without it.
       tests/distSmoke.test.ts asserts it stays off.
         The resting opacity below is what a visitor sees with JS off or under reduced motion.

       This block used to carry ~20 lines of narrative about three earlier attempts, ending in a standing
       instruction to "animate the strongest dozen and leave the haze static" if the problem came back. Both of
       those were then built and rejected — the frozen strokes read as failed ones, and the haze underlay read as
       a trail behind every line, which distSmoke now forbids outright. A comment that prescribes a rejected fix
       is worse than no comment, so the prescription is gone and the history now lives in CLAUDE.md where the
       rest of the rejections are. */
    .gate.is-up .gate-fam .gate-trail { opacity: 0.45; }

    /* THE LETTERS FAIL VISIBLE, and that is the fix for the name arriving incomplete TWICE — "I g T n" and
       then "ng Ti", a different set of letters missing each time.
         What was wrong: the rule set `opacity: 0` as the resting state and relied on a `forwards` animation to
       bring each letter back. Any letter whose animation does not run, or gets interrupted and never restarts,
       is then permanently invisible — and the thing it takes with it is the owner's NAME, on the first screen
       of his portfolio. My earlier explanation for this (frame starvation) was wrong on its own terms: CSS
       animations are driven by the document timeline, so dropped frames make them jerky, never unfinished.
       Whatever the real trigger is, the failure mode was only possible because the resting state was invisible.
         So nothing here starts at opacity 0 any more. The spring animates TRANSFORM only, and a letter whose
       animation never runs simply sits in its final position, fully legible. The same asymmetry as isCovered()
       and hasSeenGate(): decide which direction the unknown case should fail in, and make that the default. */
    .gate.is-up .gate-letter {
      animation: gate-letter 520ms cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.8, 0.2, 1) both;
      animation-delay: calc(150ms + var(--l) * 30ms);
    }
    @keyframes gate-letter {
      from { transform: translateY(36px); }
      to   { transform: none; }
    }

    /* THESE FAIL VISIBLE TOO. They had `opacity: 0` with a `forwards` fade, which is the same hazard the
       letters had — and it bit for the same reason: a screenshot arrived with the name present but the roles
       line AND the button both missing, on a modal whose only control is that button. Nothing on this screen
       may have an invisible resting state. They rise instead, from a state that is already legible. */
    .gate.is-up .gate-roles,
    .gate.is-up .gate-enter {
      animation: gate-rise 600ms cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.8, 0.2, 1) both;
    }
    .gate.is-up .gate-roles { animation-delay: 400ms; }
    .gate.is-up .gate-enter { animation-delay: 700ms; }
    @keyframes gate-rise {
      from { transform: translateY(14px); }
      to   { transform: none; }
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    /* THE FINISHED STATE, and it needs exactly one declaration.
         The driver is never built under reduced motion (`if (!reduced())` below), so no stroke ever receives an
       inline dash and there is nothing to clear — a `stroke-dasharray: none` reset used to sit here and reset
       nothing. What IS needed is the opacity pin: the per-path `stroke-opacity` attribute ramps to 1.0, so
       without this a reader with motion off gets the drawing at full strength, which is a different picture
       rather than a still of the same one. 0.45 is the envelope's plateau, i.e. where a moving reader spends
       most of the cycle.
         Two contradictory comment blocks used to be stacked here, one saying there was no dash to reset and the
       next saying the reset was back, each written in a different round and neither deleting the other. Between
       them they asserted a drift/breathe animation, a `0.3 0.7` keyframe and a 72-path count — none of which
       exist (48 strokes ship, and the only keyframes in this file are `gate-letter` and `gate-rise`). */
    .gate-trail { opacity: 0.45; }
    .gate-letter, .gate-roles, .gate-enter { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
  }

  /* Phones get half the strokes — 24 of the 48, every other curve in each family. The content is the same
     (phone work here is parity plus bug fixes), but 48 full-screen strokes animating a dash offset forever is a
     real paint cost on a handset, and at that width the thinning is invisible.
       The counts in this comment said 36 of 72 until GATE_COUNT dropped to 24 per family. Prefer describing the
     RULE ("every other curve") over the arithmetic, since the arithmetic is downstream of a dial. */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .gate-trail:nth-child(2n) { display: none; }
  }
</style>

<script is:inline define:vars={{ KEY: GATE_SEEN_KEY, MS: GATE_DISMISS_MS, UP: GATE_UP_ATTR, REVEAL: GATE_REVEAL_EVENT, DRAWN_MIN: GATE_DRAWN_MIN, PEAK: GATE_OPACITY_PEAK, RAMP: GATE_OPACITY_RAMP, OPEN_PHASE: GATE_OPEN_PHASE }}>
  // Synchronous, so a first visit covers before first paint with no flash of homepage-then-gate. Mirrors
  // BaseLayout's veil script, which is inline for the same reason.
  //
  // The policy this duplicates lives in lib/gate.ts and is unit-tested there. It is restated inline rather
  // than imported because a module script cannot run before first paint, and the whole value of the gate's
  // no-flash behaviour depends on running early. The two must agree: KEY and MS are injected from the same
  // constants, and lib/gate.ts's tests are what pin the semantics (fail closed, timer not transitionend,
  // idempotent). If you change the rule, change it there first.
  (function () {
    var gate = document.querySelector('[data-gate]');
    if (!gate) return;

    // FRESH LOAD ONLY. An inline script runs during parse, so readyState is 'loading'; when ClientRouter
    // re-executes it after a View Transition the document is already 'complete'. Without this the gate
    // raised mid-session: the script lives only in /'s HTML, so a visitor whose entry page was /research
    // (the indexed, linkable entry) is not carrying it in Astro's `scriptsAlreadyRan`, and clicking "home"
    // executed it for the first time — a full-screen interstitial over a page they were already going to.
    // BaseLayout's veil has guarded the identical hazard since it was written (`__descentVeilInit`,
    // "first load only"). isFreshLoad() in lib/gate.ts owns this rule and is tested there.
    if (document.readyState !== 'loading') return;

    var store = null;
    try { store = window.sessionStorage; } catch (e) { store = null; }

    // FAIL CLOSED: no store, or a store that throws, means do not raise. See lib/gate.ts.
    var seen = true;
    if (store) {
      try { seen = store.getItem(KEY) !== null; } catch (e) { seen = true; }
    }
    if (seen) return;

    var done = false;
    var btn = gate.querySelector('[data-gate-enter]');

    function reduced() {
      return window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
    }

    // STOPIMMEDIATEPROPAGATION, NOT JUST PREVENTDEFAULT — and the difference is the whole bug this fixes.
    // preventDefault() cancels the browser's own scrolling; it does nothing to a SIBLING listener. Deck.astro
    // also listens on window for wheel, and its onWheel does NOT check e.defaultPrevented (it checks it for
    // keydown, at Deck.astro:243, but not for wheel). So one flick while the gate was up ran the deck's
    // tween — window.scrollTo() still works under `overflow: hidden`, which by spec suppresses only the
    // USER's scrolling mechanism and leaves programmatic scrolling intact — and the visitor clicked "enter
    // the descent" to arrive in the middle of a later slide, never seeing the hero the gate exists to
    // introduce. This listener is registered at parse time and the deck's at astro:page-load, so the gate's
    // runs first and stopImmediatePropagation keeps the deck from ever seeing the event.
    function swallow(e) {
      if (done) return;
      e.preventDefault();
      e.stopImmediatePropagation();
    }

    // The deck's keys have to be swallowed for the same reason, and the previous version let every one of
    // them through: it handled Escape and Tab and returned, so ArrowDown/PageDown/space/Home/End reached
    // Deck.onKey and paged the document behind the gate.
    var DECK_KEYS = [
      'ArrowDown', 'ArrowUp', 'PageDown', 'PageUp', 'Home', 'End', ' ', 'Spacebar',
    ];

    function onKey(e) {
      if (e.key === 'Escape') { dismiss(); return; }
      if (e.key === 'Tab') {
        // A REAL TRAP, NOT A FREEZE. Blanket-blocking Tab looked like a trap but was one: clicking the
        // gate's background blurs the button to <body>, and from there a blocked Tab left a keyboard
        // visitor unable to reach the only control, with Escape the undocumented sole exit. Put focus back
        // on the button instead, so Tab cycles where there is one stop.
        e.preventDefault();
        e.stopImmediatePropagation();
        if (btn) btn.focus();
        return;
      }
      if (e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === 'Spacebar' || e.key === ' ') {
        // Let the button's own activation through, but never the deck's page-down on space.
        if (e.target === btn) return;
      }
      if (DECK_KEYS.indexOf(e.key) !== -1) {
        e.preventDefault();
        e.stopImmediatePropagation();
      }
    }

    // EVERY BODY CHILD EXCEPT THE GATE, not just <main>. `inert` on main alone left 18 focusable elements
    // reachable — measured against dist: 7 Toc links, 5 CornerNav page links, 4 CornerNav mark links
    // (GitHub / mailto / cv.pdf / LinkedIn), the nav menu button and the theme toggle, all of which render
    // AFTER </main>. The old comment claimed "the only focusable thing is the button", which was true of
    // the gate and false of the document, so the trap rested entirely on blanket-blocking Tab.
    var inerted = [];
    function setInert(on) {
      if (on) {
        var kids = document.body.children;
        for (var i = 0; i < kids.length; i++) {
          var k = kids[i];
          if (k === gate || k.tagName === 'SCRIPT' || k.hasAttribute('inert')) continue;
          k.setAttribute('inert', '');
          inerted.push(k);
        }
      } else {
        for (var j = 0; j < inerted.length; j++) inerted[j].removeAttribute('inert');
        inerted = [];
      }
    }

    function dismiss() {
      if (done) return;            // idempotent: Escape then click must not run this twice
      done = true;
      try { if (store) store.setItem(KEY, '1'); } catch (e) { /* denied; it reappears next load */ }

      gate.classList.remove('is-up');
      // The flow has nothing left to draw on once the gate is down, and a rAF loop painting a hidden overlay
      // is exactly the waste the data-gate-up flag exists to prevent elsewhere.
      if (flowRaf) { cancelAnimationFrame(flowRaf); flowRaf = 0; }
      document.documentElement.style.overflow = '';
      // Let the hero and the sky start animating: they held off while nothing they drew could be seen.
      // Order matters — clear the flag BEFORE firing, so a listener that re-reads it sees the new state.
      document.documentElement.removeAttribute(UP);
      document.dispatchEvent(new Event(REVEAL));
      setInert(false);
      window.removeEventListener('wheel', swallow, { passive: false });
      window.removeEventListener('touchmove', swallow, { passive: false });
      // `touchend` too. It was added beside the other three (the phone's only way out, see trap 3) and
      // never removed, so the handler — and through its closure the gate's whole DOM — was retained for the
      // rest of the session on any device that fired it.
      window.removeEventListener('touchend', dismiss);
      document.removeEventListener('keydown', onKey, true);
      document.removeEventListener('astro:before-swap', dismiss);

      // A TIMER, never transitionend — under reduced motion no transition fires and this would never run,
      // leaving the page scroll-locked forever. dismissMs() in lib/gate.ts owns this number.
      window.setTimeout(function () {
        // The deck measured its stops while the document was scroll-locked; make it re-measure.
        window.dispatchEvent(new Event('resize'));
      }, reduced() ? 0 : MS);
    }

    // ── THE FLOW, DRIVEN HERE RATHER THAN IN CSS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // The source's per-path animation, which this reproduces:
    //     pathLength 0.3 -> 1          the drawn dash grows from 30% of the arc to all of it, then RESETS
    //     pathOffset 0 -> 1 -> 0       the dash's leading edge travels forward, then back
    //     opacity    0.3 -> 0.6 -> 0.3 the element pulses
    //     duration   20-30s, linear, repeating forever, no delay
    //
    // THE DASH DOES NOT WRAP — IT CLIPS, and this is the detail everything else here depends on. framer-motion
    // sets pathLength="1" and writes `stroke-dasharray = "<L> <S>"` with pathSpacing defaulting to 1, so the
    // period is L+1 and always exceeds the arc: at most ONE dash is ever on the path, and the painted span is
    // [O, min(1, O+L)). Earlier rounds here assumed it tiled and reasoned in circles from that.
    //
    // EVERY path animates, and every path starts at the same point in its cycle — both are the source, and
    // deviating from either produced "lifeless ... some kids drew them in kindergarten". Animating only a subset
    // leaves the rest frozen at full length, which reads as strokes that failed rather than as depth; scattering
    // the phases replaces the source's coherent sweep with unrelated strokes caught mid-stride.
    //
    // TWO CORRECTIONS to the source, both for "entire lines go dark immediately", both at their own cause:
    //   1. the opacity envelope reaches ZERO where the drawn length resets, so the sawtooth's discontinuity has
    //      nothing visible to disrupt (opacityAt in lib/gate.ts);
    //   2. travel is CLIPPED to the arc that is actually on screen, so a stroke is never off-frame for most of
    //      its cycle (the data-a/data-b window, measured by visibleWindow at build time).
    // One substitution: durations come from a deterministic LCG rather than Math.random(), because a drawing
    // that differs between builds is against the house rule the Rules slide's seeded walk follows.
    //
    // This header previously asserted that a fixed-length dash flashes and that growth is what prevents it, and
    // that the field opens on a 4-second lead. Neither is true — the first was a wrong generalisation that went
    // on to justify two rejected schemes, and the second was superseded by the phase fraction below while the
    // old text stayed. The rejections now live in CLAUDE.md, in one place.
    // WHERE A STROKE STARTS, and the envelope, both come from lib/gate.ts via define:vars — see GATE_OPEN_PHASE
    // and opacityAt there for why 0.16 and why the envelope plateaus instead of peaking. Both are unit-tested,
    // and both are numbers this script must not be allowed to drift from.
    // HOW FAR A STROKE MAY LEAN toward warm or cool. Deliberately small: the strokes must stay near-ink, both
    // because the palette rule keeps the seal the only saturated colour in the light theme and because the point
    // is a shimmer a reader notices without being able to name.
    var TINT = 0.22;
    var SWING = 0.35;        // GATE_WIDTH_SWING in lib/gate.ts
    var TAU = Math.PI * 2;

    /**
     * A CSS colour -> [r, g, b]. HEX FIRST, and that is not defensive coding — the palette in tokens.css is
     * written in hex (`--ochre: #c8a36a`) and getPropertyValue on a custom property hands the authored string
     * straight back rather than a resolved rgb(). An rgb()-only parser silently fell through to the default
     * here, which made warm and cool both equal the base: the colour animation ran every frame and changed
     * nothing. It cost one measurement to find and would never have been visible in review.
     */
    function rgb(v) {
      var t = String(v).trim();
      var hex = /^#([0-9a-f]{3}|[0-9a-f]{6})$/i.exec(t);
      if (hex) {
        var h = hex[1];
        if (h.length === 3) h = h[0] + h[0] + h[1] + h[1] + h[2] + h[2];
        return [parseInt(h.slice(0, 2), 16), parseInt(h.slice(2, 4), 16), parseInt(h.slice(4, 6), 16)];
      }
      var m = t.match(/-?[\d.]+/g);
      return m && m.length >= 3 ? [+m[0], +m[1], +m[2]] : [239, 233, 221];
    }
    var field = gate.querySelector('.gate-field');
    var fieldCs = field ? getComputedStyle(field) : null;
    var base = rgb(fieldCs ? fieldCs.getPropertyValue('--trail-base') : '');
    var warm = rgb(fieldCs ? fieldCs.getPropertyValue('--trail-warm') : '');
    var cool = rgb(fieldCs ? fieldCs.getPropertyValue('--trail-cool') : '');

    var flowing = [];
    if (!reduced()) {
      var fams = gate.querySelectorAll('.gate-fam');
      for (var fi = 0; fi < fams.length; fi++) {
        var ps = fams[fi].querySelectorAll('.gate-trail');
        for (var k = 0; k < ps.length; k++) {
          var len = parseFloat(ps[k].getAttribute('data-len') || '0');
          var wa = parseFloat(ps[k].getAttribute('data-a') || '0');
          var wb = parseFloat(ps[k].getAttribute('data-b') || String(len));
          // The authored width, kept so the per-frame multiplier has something to multiply. Reading it back
          // from the attribute avoids restating the ramp here: gateRefPaths owns it.
          var w0 = parseFloat(ps[k].getAttribute('stroke-width') || '1');
          var seed = (fi * 36 + k) * 9301 + 49297;
          // 10-15s PER TRAVERSE. This number has now been set three times and the history is the useful part.
          //
          // The source uses 20-30s. That was measured as too slow HERE: at 20-30s a stroke advances only
          // 8-12% of its traverse during a 2.5s dwell, so the thing the dash exists to do — "lines travelling
          // through the background" — covered a tenth of its journey while anyone was looking. A landing page
          // you sit on can afford it; a gate dismissed in a gesture cannot. So it went to 6-9s, where a dwell
          // covers 28-42%.
          //
          // 6-9s then read as too fast on the owner's own look: *"let the motion be slower."* 10-15s is the
          // band between the two — a 2.5s dwell now covers 17-25%, so the sweep still reads as travel rather
          // than drift, at roughly 0.6x the previous rate. Keep it between those two measured bounds; below
          // ~6s nothing was ever asked for, and at 20s+ the motion stops registering at all.
          var dur = 10000 + ((seed % 233280) / 233280) * 5000;
          // A per-stroke phase for the weight and tint curves, from the same deterministic stream the
          // durations use. Without it the whole family would thicken and warm in unison, which reads as the
          // drawing breathing rather than as strokes each having their own weight.
          var cph = ((seed * 7919) % 1000) / 1000;
          if (len > 0) {
            flowing.push({ el: ps[k], len: len, dur: dur, ph: OPEN_PHASE, a: wa, b: wb, w0: w0, cph: cph });
          }
        }
      }
    }

    var flowRaf = 0;
    function flow(now) {
      for (var n = 0; n < flowing.length; n++) {
        var f = flowing[n];
        var t = (((now / f.dur) + f.ph) % 1);
        var drawn = (DRAWN_MIN + (1 - DRAWN_MIN) * t) * f.len;   // pathLength 0.3 -> 1 (drawnFrac in lib/gate)
        var tri = t < 0.5 ? t * 2 : (1 - t) * 2;          // pathOffset 0 -> 1 -> 0
        // THE GAP IS A WHOLE PATH LENGTH, NOT THE REMAINDER. framer-motion's `pathSpacing` defaults to 1, and
        // its units are normalised path lengths — so the source's gap is the FULL arc, making the pattern
        // period `drawn + len`. Writing the remainder instead makes the period exactly `len`, so the pattern
        // tiles the path perfectly and whatever slides off the end immediately re-enters at the start. That is
        // a second stroke segment that the source does not draw, on every curve, for the whole cycle — the
        // scattering of short disconnected ends that read as "some kids drew them in kindergarten".
        // ── TRAVEL IS CLIPPED TO THE ARC THAT IS ON SCREEN ──────────────────────────────────────────────
        // The dash does NOT wrap, and getting that wrong is what made the earlier reasoning here circular. Its
        // period is `drawn + len`, which always exceeds the arc, so at most one dash lies on the path: the
        // leading edge sits at `s` and the painted span is [s, min(len, s + drawn)], CLIPPED at the end rather
        // than re-entering at the start.
        //
        // So the sweep only has to keep that span overlapping [a, b] — the measured on-screen window. Sweeping
        // `s` from `a + m - drawn` to `b - m` does exactly that: at one extreme the dash's trailing end sits
        // just inside `a`, at the other its leading edge sits just inside `b`, and everywhere between it covers
        // more. The dash never leaves the picture, so the 42%-of-every-cycle blank is gone — and because a dash
        // END is on frame far more of the time, it reads as MORE motion, not less.
        //
        // Unclipped, `s` swept 0 -> len, and both ends of that are off-screen: the source's own opening state
        // (the first 30% of the arc) ends at 0.30 while the earliest on-screen point is 0.396, which is why the
        // reference itself starts on an empty frame.
        var m = 0.18 * (f.b - f.a);
        var lo = f.a + m - drawn;
        var hi = f.b - m;
        f.el.style.strokeDasharray = drawn + ' ' + f.len;
        f.el.style.strokeDashoffset = String(-(lo + (hi - lo) * tri));
        // ── THE ONE CHANGE THAT FIXES "entire lines go dark immediately" ────────────────────────────────
        // The source's envelope is opacity [0.3, 0.6, 0.3] — nonzero at BOTH ends of the cycle. That is the
        // whole defect: `drawn` is a sawtooth, so at the wrap it snaps from the entire arc back to a 30%
        // fragment sitting at the curve's off-screen start, and because the stroke is still at 0.3 opacity when
        // that happens the snap is fully visible. A line that was drawn across the frame is simply gone in the
        // next frame.
        //
        // Taking the envelope to ZERO at the cycle boundary moves the discontinuity to where nothing is on
        // screen to see it. Nothing else changes: same dash, same travel, same durations, same coherence. A
        // stroke now fades in, sweeps across the frame, and fades out — which is also the thing the owner was
        // reaching for with "lines travelling through the background like the trails of asteroids".
        //
        // WHY NOT THE ALTERNATIVES, since both were built and rejected: a faint full-length stroke underneath
        // stops the vanishing too, but its uncovered stretch reads as "the line leaves a trail behind"; and
        // removing the dash altogether leaves a static drawing. This touches neither — there is no second
        // layer and the motion is the approved one.
        //
        // `tri` is already 0 at t=0 and t=1 and 1 at the midpoint, so it IS the envelope; it only needed to be
        // used as a multiplier instead of as an offset from 0.3.
        // so the mid-cycle brightness survives starting from zero.
        // opacityAt() in lib/gate: ramps off zero over the first RAMP of the triangle, then holds. Zero at the
        // cycle boundary is the whole fix; the plateau is what stops the fix costing brightness.
        f.el.style.opacity = String(PEAK * Math.min(1, tri / RAMP));

        // ── WEIGHT AND COLOUR VARY AS THE STROKE TRAVELS ────────────────────────────────────────────────
        // widthAt / tintAt in lib/gate.ts own both curves and are unit-tested there; what is restated here is
        // only their arithmetic, because this script cannot import. Both are periodic in t, so neither adds a
        // discontinuity at the cycle boundary the envelope is hiding.
        //
        // SVG gives one width and one colour per path per frame, so this is variation in TIME, not along the
        // stroke. Tapering head-to-tail would need a canvas, which was built and rejected.
        f.el.style.strokeWidth = String(f.w0 * (1 + SWING * Math.sin(TAU * (2 * t + f.cph))));
        var tint = Math.sin(TAU * (2 * t + f.cph + 0.25));
        var mix = tint > 0 ? warm : cool;
        var amt = Math.abs(tint) * TINT;
        f.el.style.stroke = 'rgb(' + Math.round(base[0] + (mix[0] - base[0]) * amt) + ','
          + Math.round(base[1] + (mix[1] - base[1]) * amt) + ','
          + Math.round(base[2] + (mix[2] - base[2]) * amt) + ')';
      }
      flowRaf = requestAnimationFrame(flow);
    }
    if (flowing.length) flowRaf = requestAnimationFrame(flow);

    gate.classList.add('is-up');
    document.documentElement.style.overflow = 'hidden';
    // Raised BEFORE the canvases initialise, because this script is inline and parse-time while theirs run on
    // astro:page-load — so they see the flag on their very first check and never start a loop at all, rather
    // than starting one and being stopped a moment later.
    document.documentElement.setAttribute(UP, '');
    setInert(true);
    window.addEventListener('wheel', swallow, { passive: false });
    window.addEventListener('touchmove', swallow, { passive: false });
    // A TOUCH ANYWHERE DISMISSES, because otherwise a phone has no way out but one button.
    //
    // `touchmove` above is swallowed (trap 3: it would otherwise reach Deck.astro's sibling listener and scroll
    // the page behind the gate), and onKey only exits on Escape — a key a phone does not have. So a visitor
    // following a link on a phone, which is the arrival path for anyone coming from LinkedIn, had exactly one
    // 44px target and no gesture. `touchend` rather than `touchstart` so a scroll attempt that gets swallowed
    // still counts as "let me in" rather than firing before the finger has decided.
    window.addEventListener('touchend', dismiss, { passive: true });
    document.addEventListener('keydown', onKey, true);

    // LEAVING THE PAGE IS A DISMISSAL, and without this the listeners outlived the element they belong to.
    // ClientRouter replaces document.body on a navigation, so the gate's DOM disappears while `swallow` and
    // `onKey` — bound to window and document, which survive the swap — keep calling preventDefault forever:
    // wheel and trackpad scrolling dead on every page, Tab dead site-wide, for the rest of the session, with
    // no gate on screen to explain it. The visitor concludes their mouse broke. CLAUDE.md's Interactivity
    // contract has required "re-init on astro:page-load, WITH A TEARDOWN" all along; this is the teardown.
    document.addEventListener('astro:before-swap', dismiss);

    if (btn) {
      btn.addEventListener('click', dismiss);
      try { btn.focus({ preventScroll: true }); } catch (e) { btn.focus(); }
    }
  })();
</script>
